old i think my  grades may drop  yes yes they're very worried we get it  all right get it it's new for listen as  as hard as it is for them it's new for  everybody yeah we're all in the same  sustaining it is really hard you know i  think everybody thought going into the  summer maybe things would be better in  the fall and now here  you know a lot of kids are going through  it all over again that's the problem  there doesn't seem to be an  end in sight that's what's so concerning  this too shall pass  i believe that i do actually do believe  that  moments ago we heard from children  talking about their concerns about  returning to school during this very  unusual time in the pandemic  we're going to stick with that  psychologist and cbs news contributor  lisa damore joins us now  to answer questions and concerns from  those students lisa good morning to you  this is a really interesting  conversation and and i think  colin here in montana who we have a  question from really  sums it up when he says he's going back  to school uh  in person and like a lot of kids i think  he's con  he's concerned about the social  distancing he says he's scared his  school will feel like a jail  cold and distant what's your advice for  students confronting  that kind of experience when they go  back  well colin's right school is going to  feel different this year you know  everyone's going to be wearing masks  there's going to be plexiglass in all  sorts of new places everyone's going to  be kept at a distance from one another  but i think colin's already doing  something that will help him which is to  think about what he's walking into  because any time we're walking into a  situation that's difficult  the more we imagine it the more we  prepare ourselves mentally  the better it actually goes when we get  there so anticipating those changes and  being ready for them will make a big  difference  the other thing i would say though to  students is that  their teachers want school to be a warm  and friendly place  and i know teachers are going to do  everything in their power to make  students feel connected  and calm and cared for even while  following all of those safety  precautions part of keeping a school  a warm and friendly place can mean a  high five it can mean a hug i mean in  the old days  now we heard from one little guy gavin  age four he said when am i gonna be able  to hug my teacher again  what do you say to kids like that  well i think we tell them the truth we  say well we don't know  but until you can hug your teacher there  are lots of other ways that you can show  them  how much they mean to you and be  affectionate with them  so maybe you'll come up with some funny  little wave or some special wink that  you give each other over your masks  you can still have that kind of fun  playful  affection until we can hug again  extracurriculars are also coming up a  lot we heard from two kids who were  wondering is it safe to play sports and  then beyond sports there's a million  after-school things that kids do  what's the advice to those kids who are  weighing the risks and benefits of  getting involved with clubs with theater  all of that this is a tough one  because nothing matters more than safety  and  there may be things that kids cannot do  safely maybe sports or other kinds of  theatrical activities  but what we should tell young people is  that doesn't mean  that they can't find ways to develop  those skills to build themselves as  athletes to build themselves in the  other things they would have worked on  after school and they may even be able  to get together with their peers in a  safe way  to do those things until the more  traditional systems by which we do that  can come back  when you start talking about the older  kids high school age they're thinking  about college they're thinking about the  rest of their lives  this would be a time when they're  volunteering when they're doing things  to build their resume for t